<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="KPj0VB7AMvJ5jWTbEZljoc" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="QHjREygP1DrzrFA8KYGqOu" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="QZjT341sFnTM0vIKSwmkuK" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="BuGkMh7PJSd9zdNss6rcyf" Location="VL.GeoJSON" Version="0.0.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="PJWQxQHmHOs1KzU38QHfDi" Location="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="Hk3VViLeymhcxHmSpEP0XV" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="RxeqfEvDeMDzB8slPa9IfS">
    <Canvas Id="UmNSdat8yUzGF2H7tyPMPF" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="RDxVzlSNHiEwsitTmsdmPf">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="OHm80OAr4pRsaJpQiJES4e">
        <Canvas Id="AAA9PGn1YftMZU2ywswyn9" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="EvKjXTGeWNKEzJ3k3VjSzM" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1500" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="10 - Same place, different numbers.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ONE KNOB: PROJECTION. Drag it 0, 1, 2 and watch the world change shape.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;What is on screen is the graticule - every parallel from 80 south to 80 north, ten degrees&#xD;&#xA;apart - and two continents. THE NUMBERS NEVER CHANGE. Greenland is at the same latitudes in&#xD;&#xA;all three pictures. Only the rule for turning a latitude into a y changes, and it changes by&#xD;&#xA;one line of arithmetic.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  0  EQUIRECTANGULAR      y = lat                       every band the same height&#xD;&#xA;  1  WEB MERCATOR         y = ln(tan(pi/4 + lat/2))     bands grow towards the poles&#xD;&#xA;  2  LAMBERT EQUAL-AREA   y = sin(lat)                  bands shrink towards the poles&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;All three share the same x: longitude, straight across. Three world views, and the whole&#xD;&#xA;difference between them is which of those three functions you apply to a latitude.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WATCH GREENLAND.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  On 1 it is about the size of Africa. It is not: Africa is FOURTEEN TIMES bigger. Web&#xD;&#xA;  Mercator has to stretch the top of the world to keep angles right, and Greenland lives at&#xD;&#xA;  the top of the world.&#xD;&#xA;  On 2 Greenland shrinks to what it actually is. Areas are honest now - and the shapes have&#xD;&#xA;  gone squat, because you cannot keep both.&#xD;&#xA;  On 0 it is somewhere in between and correct in neither sense.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THAT IS THE CHAPTER: A PROJECTION IS A CHOICE ABOUT WHAT TO SACRIFICE. Angles, areas,&#xD;&#xA;distances - you may keep some. You may never keep all three. There is no neutral map, only&#xD;&#xA;maps whose distortion you have agreed to.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;AND NOW THE UNCOMFORTABLE PART. Look at 0 again.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Setting 0 is not an exotic projection. It is what you get when you take a longitude and a&#xD;&#xA;  latitude and USE THEM AS IF THEY WERE X AND Y. It has a name - equirectangular, or plate&#xD;&#xA;  carree - and it is a real projection with real distortion.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Which means every plot you have ever made by feeding lon and lat straight into a drawing&#xD;&#xA;  was already a projected map, and you had already chosen your distortion without knowing&#xD;&#xA;  you were choosing. There is no way to opt out. Only a way to know.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Flip between 1 and 2 with your eye on the band between 70 and 80 north. On Mercator it is&#xD;&#xA;  the tallest band on the map. On Lambert it is the thinnest. It is the same strip of earth.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Type your own country into one of the WKT boxes. Twelve or so lon-lat pairs is an&#xD;&#xA;  evening's work - chapter 07 and Prompt A mountain were both built that way - and then you&#xD;&#xA;  can watch YOUR country change size.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Add a fourth projection. Gall-Peters is y = sin(lat) / cos(45 deg) with x scaled by&#xD;&#xA;  cos(45 deg): one more Switch input and two more nodes. Every projection in the cylindrical&#xD;&#xA;  family is this cheap.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  coordinate system  the agreement that turns two numbers into a place&#xD;&#xA;  geographic CRS     angles on a sphere. WGS84 / EPSG:4326 - the GPS numbers&#xD;&#xA;  projected CRS      a flat sheet, in linear units. Web Mercator is EPSG:3857&#xD;&#xA;  projection         the arithmetic between them. Always a choice, always a loss&#xD;&#xA;  conformal          angles and local shape kept, area not. Setting 1&#xD;&#xA;  equal-area         area kept, shape not. Setting 2&#xD;&#xA;  equidistant        distance kept along certain lines only. No projection keeps every&#xD;&#xA;                     distance - that is a theorem, not a limitation of software&#xD;&#xA;  plate carree       the projection you get by accident. Setting 0&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  AND THE HONEST GAP: these three are written out by hand because a cylindrical projection&#xD;&#xA;  IS a line of arithmetic. Arbitrary CRS - UTM, national grids, anything by EPSG number -&#xD;&#xA;  needs a real transformation engine, and nothing in this family has one yet. Written down&#xD;&#xA;  as a gap rather than hidden. What you can already do is write any projection whose formula&#xD;&#xA;  you can find, which is more than it sounds.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  A projection is a vertex shader for the earth: same vertices in, different positions out,&#xD;&#xA;  and the choice of function is the whole aesthetic. You have been writing these for years.&#xD;&#xA;  The only new thing is that here the function has a name, a standard number, and a century&#xD;&#xA;  of arguments about which one is honest.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NO MAP IN THIS CHAPTER, on purpose: a basemap can only draw ONE projection, and this chapter&#xD;&#xA;is about there being more than one.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 08              the map hands you WGS84 - now you know which flattening it did&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 12              a raster is a field that signed a contract about where it is&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 02              the plot of ground you measured in degrees, seen honestly">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <!-- ===== the one knob ===== -->
          <Pad Id="MgcNRAIaavPrmz2sfL3lhB" Comment="PROJECTION" Bounds="-50,-60,110,20" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="1">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="Integer32" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">14</p:fontsize>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="M5xaJClTHRUXOitKZCH2fZ" Bounds="80,-63,600,45" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="&lt;- THE ONLY KNOB. Drag it between 0, 1 and 2.&#xD;&#xA;   0 equirectangular  y = lat          1 Web Mercator  y = ln(tan(pi/4 + lat/2))&#xD;&#xA;   2 Lambert equal-area  y = sin(lat)   - and x is longitude in all three.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
